From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: add the MT6897 SoC bus variant

The Wi-Fi of the MT6897 connsys ("soc7_0" in the vendor driver) has chip
ID 0x7961 and the WFDMA of a PCIe MT7921, with an MT6637 RF chip. This
adds what differs. Registers, sequences, rings and command numbers have
been compared with the vendor driver by a script that reads its source.

The bus: the registers are in a 7 MiB window of the AXI bus with another
address map, translated like the PCIe one. The rings have other numbers:
data 0, events 2, TX free events 3, commands 15.

DMA: the bus master can only reach a 20 MiB no-map region that the
bootloader reserves and opens in the EMI MPU. The DMA API can't be
pointed at it, as a restricted-dma-pool needs a region in the linear map
under its own compatible and the bootloader decides both. So this
bounces all DMA through the region in a set of dma_map_ops of its own,
as the vendor driver's copy path does. That needs ARCH_HAS_DMA_OPS,
which arm64 only selects with Xen.

Power: the connsys driver powers the common part, and the WF MCU is
brought up to its ROM here, as in the vendor driver. Its reset is a
power cycle.

The firmware: the bootloader has loaded it, so the driver hands over the
board's calibration data, front end chips and a time, and sends the
start command. There is no trailer to read the release from, so that
comes from memory. It is a different build from the MT7921 file, so what
it supports isn't known either: the vendor driver only uses the legacy
commands, and the mt76 code needs unified commands for BSS, station, key
and remain-on-channel. Whether it answers those can't be told from the
image, so this tries one and stops if it doesn't get an answer. Unless
told otherwise the driver assumes a firmware without CNM, which is what
mt7921 does without the feature flag.

The calibration data is a file with the MAC address of the device in it,
which the system has to copy from the nvdata partition.

A register access to the WF side or the WFDMA while the WF part is off
hangs the bus rather than fail. A restart that fails leaves it off, and
the common code goes on trying to use the chip after that. So while it
is off the address translation refuses those registers, the ring
registers, which the common code reaches past the bus ops, point at
memory, commands are refused and the interrupt is disabled.

mt7921_stop() returns at a command it can't send and leaves the timers
running, and with the WF part off that is the normal case here. So .stop
is its own. An interface that is down gets no .stop at all, while a reset
starts the same timers, so removal disables the delayed works before the
workqueues go away. Disabled, events and channel changes can't start them
again on the way down either.

The reset work starts over for a firmware that comes up and then stops
answering, with a power cycle and a calibration each time. That ends after
twenty restarts, each less than ten minutes after the last, with the WF
part off. Removal has to wait for the work, so it ends the tries too.

A page held by a socket keeps its place in the DMA window, so sockets can
fill it up. Refills then fail, and they only happen in NAPI, which a ring
that ran dry gets no interrupt for. A page going back to its pool does not
give up its place, so the window can't tell when to try again. A failed
mapping starts a timer instead, which schedules NAPI until refills work.

The vendor driver masks the interrupt line in the interrupt controller, and
this one in the WFDMA as PCIe does. If anything else can raise that line it
would come back at once, so the handler only takes it if the WFDMA says so.
The kernel knows what to do with a line that nobody takes care of.

Left out for now: pre-calibration backup (it calibrates at each power
on, which the vendor driver does when it has no backup), runtime power
management and system sleep, so no wake on WLAN either, the notice to the
modem of the power state and the second interrupt, the assert notice.

The debugfs switch of runtime power management is in the common code, so
it needs to do no harm. Its .fw_own gives up nothing, a reset clears the
state bit, which would leave all replies to a wake work that waits for
that reset, and removal disables its works as well.

All steps are logged, as a wrong one can hang the bus without a word.
None of this has run on hardware.
